// Dev-server config for `pnpm dev`, which is
// `tsx watch --tsconfig tsconfig.dev.json src/index.ts`.
//
// This used to be a second, hand-copied copy of the `@dripl/*` `paths` map that
// now lives in tsconfig.check.json. Two copies of a 14-entry map that only has
// to agree with each other is a drift hazard with no upside, so this file is now
// a thin overlay: it extends tsconfig.check.json and overrides only what is
// genuinely tsx's concern. `paths`, `rootDir` and the `noEmit` / `composite` /
// `incremental` triple all come from there, which means `pnpm check-types` and
// `pnpm dev` cannot resolve `@dripl/*` differently.
//
// `declaration` / `declarationMap` are inert under the inherited `noEmit: true`
// — they are kept because they are the only emit options tsx-adjacent tooling
// could read off this file, and turning them off here keeps that reading
// truthful.
//
// Inheritance of `paths` is not assumed, it is verified: `tsc --showConfig -p
// tsconfig.dev.json` prints the merged map, and `pnpm dev` boots from source on
// a tree with no dist/ anywhere. Both were checked after this rewrite.
//
// A useful side effect: this file is now a valid `tsc` config. It used to
// inherit `rootDir: "./src"` from tsconfig.json while mapping `@dripl/*` outside
// it, so `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.dev.json` emitted a wall of TS6059 and the
// only reason nobody noticed is that tsx never type-checks. Re-rooted at the
// repository root by tsconfig.check.json, the same command answers "does the dev
// path type-check?" for real.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.check.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false
  }
}
